# The expectations the retained audit evidence is checked against.
#
# Authority: docs/technical/91-verification-strategy.md § Verification registry
#            docs/technical/115-component-contract-and-schema-registry.md
#              § Accepted project boundary
# Requirements: TR-QUA-004, TR-CTR-001
# Verification: VER-FND-009-002 (forbidden-edge matrix), VER-FND-009-008 (fixture classes)
#
# This file exists because these values used to be written down twice - once in
# build/verify-registry.sh and once in tests/MechaMiner.Tools.Tests/Audit - and the
# script described its copy as "the same floor" the test asserted. It was not the same
# number, only an equal one. Measured: changing the C# assertion from 100 to 10 left the
# script at exit 0 still printing "at or above the floor of 100 that
# ArchitectureRuleTests.EveryForbiddenReferenceEdgeIsRejected asserts" - a claim about a
# number the named test no longer asserted. Two literals cannot be kept in step by a
# comment telling the next reader to keep them in step.
#
# Both readers now parse this file, and neither carries a fallback: an absent or
# unparseable value fails the reader that wanted it, rather than quietly restoring the
# number it used to hardcode. A change to either value is therefore one visible edit
# here, and the script's report is a claim about a number it actually read.
#
# Format is deliberately dull - one KEY=value per line, no quoting, no expansion, no
# `source` - so the shell parses it with sed and C# parses it with a string split, and
# neither can execute it.

# Every ordered pair of accepted projects that must NOT have a project reference,
# counting the reverse-Godot edges. This is the size of the complete matrix
# ArchitectureRuleTests.EveryForbiddenReferenceEdgeIsRejected walks over
# AcceptedArchitecture.Projects, one negative control per pair, and it is asserted
# exactly rather than as a floor.
#
# It is an exact count and not a minimum on purpose. A floor plus a footer/row
# self-consistency check - which is what verify-registry stage 3 used to be - accepts a
# hand-written inventory of 150 rows claiming 150, and accepts one of exactly 100, while
# the real matrix is 112. Neither is the matrix, and stage 3 could not tell. It cannot
# derive 112 for itself either: the number falls out of the accepted-boundary table in
# src/MechaMiner.Tools/Audit/AcceptedArchitecture.cs, and re-deriving that in shell would
# be a second implementation of the thing being checked. So the count is committed here
# and both sides compare against it - the C# test against what it actually walked, the
# script against what the evidence file carries.
#
# Changing the accepted boundary changes this number, and that is the point: adding a
# project means editing this line in the same change, which is a visible edit in the diff
# rather than an inventory that grew or shrank unremarked.
FORBIDDEN_EDGE_CONTROLS=112

# The registry failure classes TASK-FND-009-002's completion gate names, each paired with
# the rule its fixture must fail under. Space-separated `class:rule` pairs; the rule name
# is a member of MechaMiner.Tools.Audit.RegistryRule.
#
# The pairing is here rather than in the script for the same reason as the count above.
# verify-registry stage 2 used to grep for the heading prefix `## <class> (expects ` and
# nothing after it, so `## malformed (expects Whatever)` was accepted - the heading's own
# claim about which rule the fixture proves went unread. Reading the expected rule out of
# the script's own hardcoded list would have recreated exactly the two-literal defect
# this file was added to remove, since the headings are written by
# RegistryValidatorTests.EachFixtureClassFailsUnderItsOwnRule.
REGISTRY_FIXTURE_CLASSES=missing:UndefinedIdentifier duplicate:DuplicateIdentifier dangling:BrokenLink malformed:MalformedIdentifier
